When people were still working out how to hold a conversation with a computer, a keyboard and a line of text were a practical place to start. A terminal could print a reply. If I knew the command, I could ask for a list of files or send one to the printer. Knowing what to type was part of knowing how to use the machine.

The Xerox Star team described a different bargain in 1982 as [“seeing and pointing versus remembering and typing”](https://guidebookgallery.org/articles/designingthestaruserinterface/). Put a document on the screen and I can recognize it. Put a printer there too and I can point to where I want the document to go. It’s easy to see why that idea caught on. Decades later, here I am typing into a box again, this time because I can describe what I want in my own words. Does that make text the future again? I doubt it.

## What Unix and the Mac each got right

That question was open even in the Macintosh years. In [*The Anti-Mac Interface*](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/anti-mac-interface/) in 1996, Don Gentner and Jakob Nielsen asked what Apple’s interface guidelines had left out. Near the end they give Unix and the Macintosh their due in consecutive paragraphs. A programmer working with text could string Unix commands together to do something nobody had thought to put on a menu. Someone working with a handful of graphical documents could see them on a Mac desktop and move them around. Each made sense for the work people had in front of them.

Gentner and Nielsen pictured a computer you could instruct with language, examples, and pointing together. Everyday conversation seemed a stretch at the time, so they imagined a more limited language.

Speech and pointing had already been tried together. In 1980, [Richard Bolt’s *Put-That-There*](https://doi.org/10.1145/800250.807503) let people speak and point to move shapes on a display. The words could stay simple because the gesture gave the system a way to resolve what the person meant.

{% marginnote %}A transcript can also miss [what a speaker emphasizes](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1749-818X.2009.00150.x). “Move the *heading*” and “*Move* the heading” contain the same words, and the emphasis can help a listener tell what’s being corrected.{% endmarginnote %}

A chat with a language model can look like a Unix terminal. I type a line and something comes back. With a shell command, I generally know which operation I want. In chat I can ask for a brief for people who missed a meeting and work out what belongs in it as we go. [Writing about generative AI in 2023, Nielsen](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ai-paradigm/) expected people to describe outcomes and still use graphical controls to work out the details. You can see that combination in a few different forms today.

## Word and Copilot give you two places to start

In [Microsoft 365 Copilot in Microsoft Word](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/word/copilot/chat-with-copilot-about-your-word-document), the document stays in front of you. Select a paragraph and you can ask about that paragraph in the side pane. Selection has become part of how you phrase the question. You don’t have to tell the assistant where on the page to look.

