With [Office in the Microsoft 365 Copilot app](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/microsoft-copilot-blog/office-at-ignite-2026-from-apps-to-agentic-system-of-work/4572226), you can go the other way. Start with a request in chat, then click into the Word document it opens alongside the conversation and edit it yourself. That reversal interests me because sometimes I only find out what I’m making once I’ve started.

Word has always been good at giving me room for that. I can start with a few names, put prices beside them, and make a table when the columns get hard to follow. A few more rows and I find myself wanting to sort by price and work out a total. Somewhere along the way my Word file became a spreadsheet. On another day I might draw the seating plan beside the names and find that the drawing is the part I want to keep.

I’d like that latitude in an AI interface. Opening Word makes a small bet that I’m making a document. Opening a chat makes one too. It assumes the first thing I type is addressed to someone. Sometimes I just want to put a few words down and see where they take me.

## Speech and sketches in the same workspace

In [SketchGPT](https://zaynehuang.github.io/SketchGPT/), a prototype presented at UIST 2025, you can draw directly over an app while speaking. In the paper’s travel example, someone traces the route they have in mind and sketches a table for the itinerary. They explain aloud what should go in it. The system turns the request into a list of proposed actions for the person to check before it takes them.

