The sketch can say where the table should go, and the voice can say what it should contain. I can imagine getting an answer faster by doing both at once. The confirmation step also gives me somewhere to look if the system mistakes a rough mark for something I wanted it to draw.

## Moving things by asking and by hand

The [VR Mover paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/3746059.3747673) identifies Bolt’s *Put-That-There* as its closest precedent. In [one VR Mover demo](https://vr-mover.github.io/), you can point out three destinations in a virtual room and tell the system to move three chairs there. The researchers found that participants liked speech and pointing for broad moves and could still reach for the manipulation handles or use virtual hands for finer adjustments.

